ibn Makhzūm ibn Ṣāhilah ibn Kāhil al-Kāhilī. Al-Marzubānī said: He was a nobleman, a poet, and one who lived in both the pre-Islamic and Islamic periods. He is the one who said:

We kept it from the ʿAbāhilah;
I summon Banū ʿAmr, and I summon Ṣāhilah.

Al-Tamīmī, one of Banū ʿĀmir of Banū Kaʿb ibn Saʿd ibn Zayd Manāh ibn Tamīm. He lived into Islam, passing through both the pre-Islamic and Islamic periods. He exchanged satirical verse with al-Aghlab al-ʿIjlī, the rajaz poet mentioned earlier under alif in the first section. Al-Marzubānī mentioned him in Muʿjam al-Shuʿarāʾ and stated that Huraym encountered him at the market of ʿUkāẓ and said to him: ‘Ugly in forelock and nape;
a slave who floats when the people sink;
fa-mā ṣafā ʿaduwwukum wa-lā ṣafā [sense unresolved];
ka-mā shirāru al-baql aṭrāfu al-safā [sense unresolved].’ Al-Aghlab asked him, ‘Who are you, woe to you?’ He replied: ‘I am a young man of Banū Muqāʿis, those who strike the skulls of horsemen’—within a longer set of verses.
